hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well the psychologist  Lawrence Kohlberg and a number of  different papers and books set out a  theory of human moral development a very  comprehensive theory that distinguishes  six stages and this is often brought up  in the context of ethics in part because  we can associate certain moral theories  or ethical perspectives with these  different stages and there's a sort of  valorisation it's it's not good for a  person to remain down here where they do  indeed have to start the ideal is for  them to advance all the way up to level  six Kohlberg himself in point of fact  does not think that that happens in the  case of most people with in most  cultures and institutions but he is  setting this out as a sort of blueprint  for how a person would become a  progressively better and better human  being and all of this has to do not just  with what they would do as a matter of  fact he points out that a person might  be able to carry out moral reasoning  without being able to necessarily  fulfill what that moral reasoning  requires and that's  for an issue rather it sits in terms of  the ways in which we think out the  situation's dilemmas problems issues  that we encounter and what we bring to  bear what values we have what what  strategies what things we consider to be  relevant or important in making moral  decisions and one last thing before we  look at each of these stages in turn  Kohlberg believes that not only can you  find these across cultures a person  cannot skip stages now they could be  forced into a situation or institution  where they're supposed to behave and  recognize a higher stage and they might  actually be able to function for a  little while  in that context but in general in order  to develop you have to start at stage  one you can't skip that all the way up  to stage five you have to go into stage  two or level two and then go to the next  level three four five and all the way up  to the top you cannot jump through the  the ladder so to speak there's no way of  shortcutting the process and it happens  through not only maturation but also  through moral conflicts and having to  think these things out and reflecting on  one's own experiences which are quite  often experiences of failure so all that  said let's talk about these six stages  now and Kohlberg associates them into  three groups we have what he calls a  preconventional level or stage which  includes number one and number two then  we have a conventional one where social  conventions the mores of the society or  culture group or even perhaps even  family if we're talking about isolated  individuals becomes very important and  that includes three and four and then we  have a post conventional which doesn't  mean that convention  are entirely left behind but they are  placed into a larger context in which  conventions make sense in terms of and  in how they contribute to something that  goes beyond something that transcends  mere conventions so stage one and I'm  taking this from moral development a  review of the theory stage one he said  he calls us the punishment and obedience  orientation a person who is at this  stage focuses primarily on the physical  and perhaps to some degree emotional  consequences of action  those determine the goodness or badness  of an action so if an action gets me  punished it's a bad action if the  punishment if it action gets me rewarded  it is a good action or it could be good  if it doesn't involve any sort of  punishment that I'm taking he says that  this this is looked at this way  regardless of the human meaning or value  of those consequences so all those are  put aside for somebody who's at level  one he goes on to say that avoidance of  punishment unquestioning deference to  power are valued in their own right not  in terms of anything else and he  specifies that at the level four there's  also this respect for authority but  that's very different than what's  happening at level one and level one you  respect authority because they can  punish you and level four there's a more  abstract recognition that Authority  itself is good in some way not just  because they can punish you  some people will remain at level one  their entire life most people Kohlberg  thinks are able to move on to level two  or stage 2 what he calls the  instrumental relativist orientation and  here we're looking at things through the  lens of what's good for me so it's very  egoistic and in terms of what's  instrumentally good for me what's  instrumentally good becomes good what's  instrumentally bad what stands  the way of my desires becomes bad and I  start to take other people into  consideration I start to think about  their needs but primarily just insofar  as they have to do with my needs so he  gives the example of saying I'll scratch  your back if you scratch mine it's not  quite the same thing as a recognition of  reciprocity that's more just saying this  is a useful strategy for both of us to  get what we want and there's no value of  the other person as such in an  orientation along these these sorts of  lines so he says human relations are  viewed in terms like that of the  marketplace today we might call that a  transactional way of looking at things  that's a very common term that's being  used today for ethics transactional  ethics somebody who's got transactional  ethics pretty low level of moral  development then we move into the  conventional levels and here there's  kind of a interesting leap that takes  place so he says at this level  maintaining expectations of the  individuals family group or nation  becomes perceived as valuable in its own  right prior to that it was just listen I  want to you know get the stuff I want or  I just want to avoid being punished so  I'll say the party line now the person  actually starts to believe it and they  start to use that in their moral  reasoning so the attitude is not only  one of conformity to personal  expectations and social order but of  loyalty to it valuing it actively  maintaining supporting and justifying  the order and he says there's two stages  involved here the lower stage is what  he's got a couple names for this  interpersonal concordance or you know  harmony with other people right and he  calls this the good boy or a nice girl  orientation good behavior is what that  which pleases or helps others and is  approved by them there's he says there's  much conformity to stereotypical images  of what is majora  or natural behavior behaviors judged by  intention saying a person means well  becomes important for the first time and  you win approval by being nice to other  people by satisfying the social  conventions which now are come you know  to be valued in their own right so a  people-pleaser is at this level and we  might think that certain ethics could  also be at this level if your ethics is  primarily based on how you make other  people feel an ethics of sympathy for  example then that would probably be at  level three at level four Kohlberg  thinks we have a higher stage of  development because now we're starting  to grasp abstract things no longer  within the matrix of relationships but  for their their own sake for the first  time as things that are valuable in  their own sake so he says because it's  the law &amp; order' orientation he says  there's an orientation towards Authority  fixed rules the maintenance of the  social order right behavior consists of  doing one's duty showing respect for  authority and maintaining the given  social order following the rules not  breaking them maybe even going beyond  that and thinking about what the rules  really require of you so you can be  conscientious and here there's kind of  an interesting bifurcation it's its  rules but it's also the person who  happens to be in charge you might say  well what if there's a conflict there  well then you've got a level 4 issue  going on right now by moving past the  the preconventional and conventional  levels we start to get into the realm of  where Kohlberg thinks real human moral  development is taking place many people  do manage to get into the the social  convention phase level 3 and level 4 but  not too many people actually make it to  5 and 60 things so at this level there's  an effort to define moral values and  principles that have a lid  apart from the authority or emotional  tenor of groups things that are more  Universal things that are more abstract  things that you could say are part of  the civilization rather than just part  of a culture so he calls the first one a  social contract legalistic orientation  and he says generally with utilitarian  overtones what does that mean to have  utilitarian overtones it means that you  think about how things affect the  community as a whole and you look at  decisions in a rather flexible way  saying we want to maximize the good  outcomes and minimize the bad outcomes  that means that some people in fact may  have some bad outcomes imposed on them  but we only do that for the sake of  maximizing the total good that is going  to be produced and very often this will  be framed in terms of respecting  individual rights or people having  individual rights norms agreed to by the  society rather than norms that just  happen to be their right norms that a  reasonable person could come up with  that's why it's a social contract theory  there is as I pointed out that  utilitarian overtone to it he says  there's a clear awareness of the  relativism of personal values and  opinions and a corresponding emphasis on  rules for reaching consensus it's no  longer just rules that you have to obey  because the rules it's now the rules  themselves serve a purpose in allowing  us to work out our problems with each  other they become procedural rules as  opposed to substantive rules at level  six which usually in in Kohlberg's  theory he did theorized about a level  seven in a few places level six is  usually viewed as the pinnacle of his  theory causes the universal ethical  principle orientation he tells us that  right is defined by the decision of  conscience in accord with notice this  this key idea self chose  and ethical principles appealing to  logical comprehensiveness universality  and consistency so that's quite a  mouthful  think about what that actually means  Universal ethical principles that are  chosen by the person this cannot be a  frivolous or arbitrary choice because  the universal ethical principles that  are chosen do have to themselves meet  these standards logical  comprehensiveness universality  consistency they have to cover all the  cases they have to be consistent with  each other so you can't just pick  principles willy-nilly  for example Kohlberg would not say that  the marquis de sade and the characters  that he places his philosophy in the  mouth of in his novels is a level six  guy because he's you know got these  Universal ethical principles he does  actually have Universal ethical  principles but they're not really  ethical principles that would be  logically consistent and could be  applied across the board instead what  are some examples he he says the Golden  Rule the categorical imperative Conchi  and morality for example which would fit  in there he says they're not concrete  rules like the Ten Commandments at heart  these are universal principles of  justice reciprocity and equality of  human beings and respect for the dignity  of human beings as individual persons so  you see there's quite a difference  between this and everything that's going  on at a lower level and the person has  to be able to choose these principles  for themselves  so for Kohlberg there is a full  development of the human being and going  from level 1 all the way up to level 6  or even to level 5 that's pretty good in  his book there are some criticisms that  have been made of the theory but it is  quite an interesting stage development  model and as I pointed out how do you  move from from you know place to place  within this through experiences through  talking with others through  failures and reflecting on those through  conflicts and eventually through  changing what it is that you value and  how you think about moral  decision-making  you
 

